Chapter 5, Section 9 lists "kei" as the terminator for nu, but it's not only for nu, but for all of NU.

Chapter 5, Section 14, page 104 says "dadylsi" for "dadysli".

Chapter 5, section 14, page 105 has the lujvo "cpumi'i" but defines it at the bottom of the set as "cpami'i".

Chapter 5, section 14, page 106 says "plise tapla" for "apple cake". Does Turkish really refer to a cake as a tile, or is this a misunderstanding of the definition of "tapla"?

Chapter 5, section 14, page 108 says "tumla spisa". This should probably be "tumla pagbu", since pieces of land are not actually detached.

Chapter 5, section 14, page 109 says "rostu mojysu'a". "rostu" is not a Lojban gismu, nor an Old Loglan primitive. It's supposed to be "mrostu". mi'e sarefo

Chapter 5, section 14, page 112 lists numerous tanru examples whose meanings are hypernyms of the tertau instead of hyponyms. This contradicts the primariness of the tertau as defined on page 84. The word "ja" should probably be inserted between the constituents, or the entire section should be removed.

